# Learn Brief in Y Minutes

Brief is a strict markup language designed to replace Markdown for documents
written by humans and consumed by humans *and* LLMs. It has three goals:

1. *One way to do it.* Every construct has a single canonical spelling.
   Ambiguous input is a compile error, not a guess.
2. *Token economy.* The source and the rendered LLM output are designed
   to be cheaper than equivalent Markdown.
3. *Real compiler.* Errors come with source spans, error codes, and help
   text, like `rustc`. There are no warnings — just success or failure.

There is no inline HTML. The single extension point is *shortcodes*.

The whole language fits in one file. Read it top to bottom.

## 1. Comments

Line comments start with `//` and must occupy the whole line. They are
stripped before any output.

Block comments use C-style `/* */` delimiters. They cannot nest, and the
opening `/*` and closing `*/` must each appear at the start or end of a
line — never in the middle of text.

## 2. Headings

Brief supports six levels of headings, written with `#` through `######`,
followed by exactly one space and the heading text.

```brief
# Heading level 1
## Heading level 2
### Heading level 3
#### Heading level 4
##### Heading level 5
###### Heading level 6
```

Seven or more `#` is an error: B0301 "heading level exceeds maximum of
6". Setext headings (`===` / `---` underlines) do NOT exist. Two spaces,
zero spaces, or no text after the `#` run are all errors.

## 3. Paragraphs and line breaks

Multiple consecutive non-blank lines that don't begin with a block sigil
get joined with a single space when rendered. A blank line separates
paragraphs. A trailing backslash at the end of a line is the hard-break
marker — it forces a line break inside a single paragraph.

```brief
This is one paragraph
that spans two lines.

This is the next paragraph.\
And this line breaks within it.
```

## 4. Inline emphasis

Each kind of emphasis uses a SINGLE-character marker on each side:

- `*bold*` renders as strong
- `_italic_` renders as em
- `+underline+` renders as underline
- `~strike~` renders as strikethrough

Doubled markers (`**bold**`, `__italic__`) are NOT valid. Brief refuses
them rather than treating them as a different style — this is the entire
point.

```brief
A line with *bold*, _italic_, +underline+, and ~strikethrough~ words.

*bold with _italic inside_ and back to bold*
```

Emphasis kinds nest freely with each other, but the SAME kind cannot
nest (that would be B0204). Multi-line emphasis is also an error
(B0205).

A marker flanked by alphanumerics on both sides is treated as a literal
character, not as emphasis:

```brief
snake_case_name              // literal — no italic
file_with_underscores.txt    // literal
3*4 = 12                     // literal asterisks
```

To force a marker to be literal in a position where it would otherwise
be emphasis, escape it with `\`:

```brief
This costs \*5\* dollars.
```

## 5. Inline code

Use the `printf` function. To include a literal backtick inside inline
code, switch to the double-backtick form. This is the ONLY exception in
the whole language to the "one canonical form" rule, and it exists
because you cannot escape inside a verbatim region.

```brief
Run ``echo `whoami` `` to print the user.
```

## 5a. Definition lists

Definition lists pair a term with its definition. They are uncommon
enough that Brief expresses them as a block shortcode, `@dl`, rather
than growing the top-level grammar.

```brief
@dl
Term 1
: Definition of term 1.
Term 2
: Definition of term 2, which can
  span multiple lines via continuation.
@end
```

The `: ` marker is exact: colon followed by one space. Two spaces, no
space, or any other character after the colon is a B0505 error.
Continuation lines align to the content column of `: ` (parent indent
plus two spaces). Multiple definitions per term are not supported in
v0.3 — two `: ` lines under one term is a B0505 error.

A live definition list:

@dl
Term 1
: Definition of term 1.
Term 2
: Definition of term 2.
@end

## 6. Lists

Two kinds — unordered and ordered — and they do NOT mix at the same
level. Switching between them ends the previous list.

The unordered marker is `-` followed by exactly one space. `*` and `+`
are NOT valid unordered markers.

```brief
- first item
- second item
- third item
```

The ordered marker is digits, then `.`, then exactly one space.
Numbering MUST be sequential starting from 1. Out-of-order is an error
(B0501) — the renderer does not silently renumber for you.

```brief
1. step one
2. step two
3. step three
```

Nested lists use exactly TWO spaces of indentation per level. One space,
three spaces, or tabs are all errors.

```brief
- top level
  - nested
    - deeper still
  - back to nested
- another top
```

You can mix list types between levels:

```brief
1. ordered top
  - bullet inside
  - another bullet
2. ordered top again
```

A list item can have continuation lines, indented to match the content
column (the marker plus its space):

```brief
- this item has
  a continuation line
- next item
```

Unordered list items can carry a task-state marker as their first
content. The marker is exactly `[x] ` (done) or `[ ] ` (todo) — strict
literal: lowercase `x`, single space inside the brackets, single space
after. Anything else (uppercase `X`, `[  ]` with two spaces, `[x]`
without a trailing space) is plain text.

```brief
- [x] ship v0.4
- [ ] write release notes
- plain bullet, not a task
```

HTML emit renders these as a checkbox `<input type="checkbox" disabled>`
inside `<li class="task-list-item">`, with the parent `<ul>` carrying
`class="contains-task-list"` if any item is a task. LLM emit preserves
the marker verbatim. Task markers are NOT supported on ordered list
items — `1. [x] foo` is just text.

## 7. Blockquotes

A blockquote starts with `>` followed by exactly one space. Subsequent
lines beginning the same way continue it. Nested blockquotes use
additional `>` markers. A blank line ends the blockquote.

```brief
> An outer quote.
>> A nested quote.
> Back to the outer.
```

## 8. Code blocks

Triple-backtick fences only. Tilde fences (`~~~`) and four-space-indent
code blocks are NOT supported. The opening fence may include a language
tag. Content between the fences is verbatim — no escaping, no inline
parsing. Unterminated fences are an error (B0305) at end of file.

For example, here is a real fenced Rust block:

```rust
fn main() {
    println!("hi");
}
```

## 9. Horizontal rule

Exactly three dashes, on a line by themselves. Two or four dashes are
errors.

---

## 10. Tables

Tables are introduced by the `@t` directive. The first `|`-row is the
header. There is NO separator row.

@t
| Name | Age | City
| John | 30 | NYC
| Jane | 25 | LA

The trailing `|` is optional. Cells are trimmed of leading and trailing
whitespace. Every row must have the same number of cells as the header
— mismatches are an error (B0502).

A `|` inside a `` ` ``-delimited or ``` `` ```-delimited inline code span
is not a cell separator. To put a literal `|` outside a code span into a
cell, escape it as `\|`.

Column alignment is set on the directive:

@t(align: [left, right, center])
| Name | Score | Status
| Ada | 1024 | active
| Bob | 42 | idle

Alignment array length must match the column count (B0504).

## 11. Shortcodes — the single extension point

Shortcodes are how Brief replaces inline HTML. Two forms exist: an
inline form `@name(args)[content]` and a block form that opens with
`@name(args)`, has a body, and closes with `@end`.

Arguments inside `(...)` are positional or keyword (mixed allowed,
positionals first). Argument values are bare idents, integers,
double-quoted strings, or arrays. The `[content]` of an inline
shortcode is parsed as inline. Block bodies are parsed as full blocks
(recursively). All shortcodes must be REGISTERED — using an unknown
one is an error (B0401).

Built-in shortcodes that ship with the reference compiler:

@t
| Name | Form | Purpose
| link | inline | hyperlink
| image | inline | image
| kbd | inline | keyboard input
| sub | inline | subscript (replaces inline `<sub>`)
| sup | inline | superscript (replaces inline `<sup>`)
| details | block | collapsible block (replaces `<details>/<summary>`)
| t | block | tables (special-cased)
| code | block | same as triple-backtick fence
| callout | block | boxed note
| math | both | LaTeX math, inline or block
| footnote | inline | footnote text
| ref | inline | cross-document reference (path#anchor → display text)

Inline HTML has no general escape hatch in Brief. The four built-ins
above (`@kbd`, `@sub`, `@sup`, `@details`) cover the inline tags that
came up most often in real documents. `@br` is not provided — use `\`
at end of line (§12) for hard breaks. Anything else means the document
shouldn't be inline-HTML in the first place; if you must, register a
custom shortcode in `brief.toml`.

An inline link — note the markdown-style `[text](url)` sugar that
supplies the URL as positional arg #1:

See @link[the spec](https://example.com/brief) for details.

An image with required keyword args (inline, embedded in a paragraph):

The architecture is shown here: @image(src: "diagram.png", alt: "architecture diagram")[].

Keyboard input: press @kbd[Ctrl+C] to abort.

Inline math: the energy is @math[E = m c^2].

A footnote: this claim is contested.@footnote[See Smith, 2025.] The
inline form is the only form — definitions live in the `[...]` body next
to the reference, never elsewhere in the file. The compiler auto-numbers
them in document order and emits a list at the end (HTML `<ol
class="footnotes">`, LLM `[^1]: ...`).

A block callout — the `kind` argument is constrained to one of `info`,
`warning`, or `danger`; anything else is B0404 BadEnumValue.

@callout(kind: warning)
Watch out for the @kbd[Shift+Delete] shortcut on Linux.
It can wipe data without going through the trash.
@end

A cross-document reference — the body holds the target `path#anchor`
verbatim and the parens supply the display text as positional arg #1
(the inverse of `@link`'s sugar, because here the *target* is the
unforgiving part):

```brief
See @ref[guide.brf#install](installation) for the steps.
```

The path is project-relative (rooted at the directory containing
`brief.toml`) and must end in `.brf`. Leading `/`, `..` segments, and
backslash separators are rejected (B0603). Missing target files are
B0601, missing anchors are B0602, and if no `brief.toml` can be found
by walking up from the source file, every `@ref` is B0604. The body
must be a plain path — emphasis and nested shortcodes inside it are
errors. In LLM mode the cross-document link is dropped and only the
display text remains.

Custom shortcodes are registered in `brief.toml` (see §13).
User-defined shortcodes can NEVER be self-terminating — only `@t` and
the triple-backtick fence are allowed that special form.

## 12. Escapes

`\` escapes the next character, treating it as a literal. `\\` produces
a literal backslash. Escapes are honored everywhere EXCEPT inside code
spans and code blocks, which are verbatim by definition.

```brief
\*not bold\* \[not a bracket\] \\\\backslash
```

## 13. Compiling

```
brief compile doc.brf --target=html
brief compile doc.brf --target=llm --report-tokens
brief compile doc.brf --target=json
```

The `--target=llm` mode produces a deterministic, token-minimized
rendering for inclusion in LLM prompts. Comments are stripped, blank
lines are collapsed, indentation drops to one space per level, emphasis
markers are preserved (use `--strip-emphasis` to drop them), and
shortcodes render to compact forms:

- `@link[text](url)` becomes `[text](url)`
- `@image(src, alt)` becomes `[image: alt]` (`--keep-asset-urls` preserves the URL)
- `@callout(kind: warning) ...` becomes `[!warning] ... [/!]`
- `@math[x^2]` becomes `$x^2$`
- `@footnote[body]` becomes `[^N]` inline; definitions are appended at
  the end of the document as `[^N]: body` lines
- `@ref[path#anchor](text)` becomes just `text` — the cross-document
  link is dropped because it has no meaning in a flattened LLM context

`--report-tokens` prints a stderr line with a cl100k character/token
estimate. The estimate is best-effort and not normative.

## 14. Configuration: brief.toml

At the project root, place a `brief.toml`. It declares custom
shortcodes, enables strictness flags, and points to templates.

```toml
[project]
name = "my-docs"
version = "0.1.0"

[compile]
strict_heading_levels = true
default_target = "html"

[shortcodes.note]
kind = "block"
template_html = "<aside class=\"note\">{{content}}</aside>"
template_llm = "[note] {{content}} [/note]"

[shortcodes.callout]
kind = "block"
arguments = { kind = { type = "string", required = true,
  oneof = ["info", "warning", "danger"] } }
template_html = "templates/callout.html"
```

Templates are minimal mustache: only `{{content}}` (rendered children)
and `{{args.name}}` (argument values). No conditionals, no loops, no
embedded code. Logic belongs in build hooks, not templates.

## 15. Diagnostics and error codes

Errors come out in `rustc` form:

```
error[B0204]: emphasis cannot nest with the same marker
  --> doc.brf:14:23
   |
14 | This is *outer *inner* outer*.
   |        ^^^^^^^ inner emphasis re-uses the `*` marker
   |
   = help: use a different emphasis marker for the inner span,
           e.g. _outer *inner* outer_
```

Code ranges:

- B01xx — lexical (tabs, BOM, encoding)
- B02xx — inline parsing (emphasis, code spans)
- B03xx — block parsing (headings, fences, comments)
- B04xx — shortcode resolution (unknown, type, enum, form)
- B05xx — semantic / cross-cutting (list sequence, table columns)
- B06xx — cross-document references (missing file, missing anchor,
  bad target, no project root)

`brief explain B0204` prints the long-form explanation. There is no
`--allow-warnings`. There never will be.

## 16. The whole list of things that are intentionally NOT here

- Inline HTML — use a shortcode.
- `**bold**` / `__italic__` — one marker on each side; period.
- `*` or `+` for bullets — only `-` is valid.
- Setext headings — only `#`-prefixed.
- Indented code blocks — use triple-backtick fences.
- Reference-style links — use `@link[text](url)`.
- Table separator row — `@t` header is implicit.
- Renumbering ordered lists — 1, 2, 3 or it's an error.
- Tab characters anywhere — two-space indent only.
- Inline trailing comments — comments are whole lines.

If a thing isn't on this page, it isn't in the language.

